// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/library-from-a-cdn.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipeLibraryFromACdn(path: "/recipes/library-from-a-cdn", title: "Load a library from a CDN — Recipes", description: "To use a library from another origin, list its URL in `meta.scripts`.") {
    RecipeShell("Load a library from a CDN", summary: "To use a library from another origin, list its URL in `meta.scripts`. The compiler never fetches anything, so it cannot read a file on another origin; your own scripts call the library instead.  This config loads Chart.js and pins the file with its integrity hash:  ```json \{ \"meta\": \{ \"scripts\": [\"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4.4.1/dist/chart.umd.min.js\"], \"integrity\": \{ \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/chart.js@4.4.1/dist/chart.umd.min.js\": \"sha384-…\" \} \} \} ```  This script draws a chart with it:  ```js // src/charts.js /**  * A bar chart of `values`, one bar a quarter.  * @param \{HTMLCanvasElement\} canvas  * @param \{number[]\} values */ function drawSales(canvas, values) \{ return new Chart(canvas, \{ type: \"bar\", data: \{ labels: [\"Q1\", \"Q2\", \"Q3\", \"Q4\"], datasets: [\{ label: \"Sales\", data: values \}] \}, \}); \} ```  This page hands the script a canvas, and destroys the chart when the page leaves:", outputs: [], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Call WF from a script", to: "/recipes/call-wf-from-a-script"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Call your own JavaScript", to: "/recipes/call-your-own-script"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Draw a chart from a library", to: "/recipes/chart-from-a-library")], source: "md-docs/recipes/library-from-a-cdn.md", how: true, hasVariations: false) {
        code {
            CodeBlock(#"page Sales(path: "/sales", title: "Sales", description: "How it is going.") {
    state values: [Number] = [12, 19, 7, 15]

    Heading("Sales").h1
    Host(tag: "canvas",
         mount: (c) => drawSales(c, values),
         cleanup: (chart) => chart.destroy())
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Markdown("How an entry in `meta.scripts` loads:\n\n- **In order.** The build links each entry with `<script defer>` in the order listed, before your own scripts and the compiled code. A library is there when the code that uses it runs.\n- **Out of order.** `\{ \"src\": \"…\", \"async\": true \}` loads a script that nothing on the page waits for (analytics, a tag manager) with `<script async>`. Deferred scripts run in order, so a large one listed plainly would hold back the compiled code, and the page's first full paint, until it arrived. An `async` script runs whenever it arrives instead. A module, or a script whose `globals` your `.wf` code calls, cannot be `async` (`E111`).\n- **After the page.** `\{ \"src\": \"…\", \"load\": \"after\" \}` puts no tag in the HTML at all: the runtime requests the script once the page has loaded and drawn its first frame. A browser fetches everything a page names while it loads, and a page's speed is measured against what it asked for before it first painted. A large analytics tag named in the HTML can cost a phone's Largest Contentful Paint more than a second, even with `async`. The script's origin still joins the policy. A reader who leaves within the first second is not counted. The same limits as `async` apply (`E111`).\n- **Pinned.** `meta.integrity` pins the file to a hash. A library from another origin without a hash draws a `D05` warning.\n- **Allowed by the policy.** The build widens the Content-Security-Policy by exactly the origins listed, so the policy that ships beside a declared library never blocks it.\n- **As a module.** `\{ \"src\": \"https://…/confetti.mjs\", \"module\": true, \"as\": \"Confetti\" \}` imports an ES module and puts its exports on `window.Confetti`. Most CDNs also serve a plain build, which needs neither key.\n- **Called from `.wf` directly.** `\{ \"src\": \"…\", \"globals\": [\"Chart\"] \}` puts the names a library defines in scope, each typed `Any`, because the compiler cannot see their shape. A class among them is constructed when called.")

    }
}
